@typeparam TItem

@* Leading empty/null option so a null or unmatched-default value shows blank instead of silently
   displaying the first option while the model holds null -- without this, the browser selects the
   first <option> whenever nothing is explicitly marked selected= (no JS attached yet on
   prerender/tests), while the model still holds null. Gated by the host: a nullable enum's
   _isNullable, or a string select's ShowNullOption (suppressed when the consumer opts out via
   NullOptionText=null, or TValue can't represent "no value" -- a non-nullable value type). *@
@if (ShowNullOption)
{
    <option class=@($"edit-select-option {CssClass}")
            value=""
            selected=@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(CurrentValueAsString))
            id=@($"{Id}-option-none")
            data-test-id=@($"{Id}-option-none")>
        @NullOptionText
    </option>
}
@* An unmatched current value (e.g. an underlying int with no defined enum member, an untouched
   default like 0, or a stale model value not in Options) must still display blank -- otherwise the
   browser silently selects the first real option while the model holds something else, the exact
   mismatch the leading blank option was added to fix (bc67ec0). A hidden+disabled placeholder shows
   blank, is not user-selectable, and never submits. Host computes ShowPlaceholder so it only renders
   when the leading null option above isn't already covering the "no match" case. *@
@if (ShowPlaceholder)
{
    @* A selected hidden option still supplies the closed <select>'s displayed text in every browser,
       which is what makes this the model-declared [Placeholder]/[Display(Prompt)] slot for a native
       select (see EditSelectEnum/EditSelectString). PlaceholderText is null unless the host resolved
       one from the bound property's attributes, and @PlaceholderText renders nothing for a null value,
       so the DOM stays byte-identical to before this parameter existed -- committed visual-regression
       baselines depend on that when no placeholder attribute is present. *@
    <option class=@($"edit-select-option {CssClass}")
            value=""
            disabled
            hidden
            selected
            id=@($"{Id}-option-placeholder")
            data-test-id=@($"{Id}-option-placeholder")>@PlaceholderText</option>
}
@foreach (var o in Options)
{
    <option title=@Tooltip
            class=@($"edit-select-option {CssClass}")
            value=@ValueString(o)
            selected=@(Equals(ValueString(o), CurrentValueAsString))
            id=@($"{Id}-option-{o.ToId()}")
            data-test-id=@($"{Id}-option-{o.ToId()}")>
        @DisplayText(o)
    </option>
}
